From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Alejandro Garcia <skipper.pelygro@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 28 Oct 2026 22:28:09 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] arm64: dts: exynos: google: gs201-panther: pin LDO25M at 1.8
 V

LDO25M is the touch controller's VDD. It has no voltage here, so the
regulator core leaves it at whatever the PMIC holds. Google's common
gs201-pmic.dtsi constrains it to 1.6-1.95 V, panther's
gs201-panther-pmic.dtsi doesn't narrow that, and ABL leaves it at
1.8 V, which vdd-ts reads on the phone. Fix it at 1.8 V, as the Pixel
Fold describes its own LDO25M, so the touch controller gets stock's
voltage whatever the PMIC held before.

Tested on a Pixel 7 from slot B (7.3.0-rc6): vdd-ts reads 1.8 V, with the
touch controller as its only user, and the controller probes as before.

Suggested-by: Derek Ross <derekross@gmail.com>
Assisted-by: Claude Opus 5.5
Signed-off-by: Alejandro Garcia <skipper.pelygro@gmail.com>
---
 arch/arm64/boot/dts/exynos/google/gs201-panther.dts | 4 +++-
 1 file changed, 3 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/exynos/google/gs201-panther.dts b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/exynos/google/gs201-panther.dts
index 1c36f6112..27e20f56b 100644
--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/exynos/google/gs201-panther.dts
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/exynos/google/gs201-panther.dts
@@ -131,9 +131,11 @@ pmic-1 {
 		status = "disabled";
 
 		regulators {
-			/* No voltage: the PMIC keeps the 1.8 V it holds. */
+			/* Stock's 1.8 V, which ABL leaves programmed */
 			ldo25m: ldo25m {
 				regulator-name = "vdd-ts";
+				regulator-min-microvolt = <1800000>;
+				regulator-max-microvolt = <1800000>;
 			};
 
 			/*
